WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.680
To understand what's happening with Greece
and the Eurozone, think about a dinner party.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.299
If you're cooking just for yourself and your
spouse, it's easy: you make something you

00:00:08.299 --> 00:00:13.880
both like. But if you've got guests, things
get harder. If you need to accommodate a vegetarian,

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.930
and someone who is gluten free, and someone
with a soy allergy, your options get really

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.779
limited. And that's the problem with Europe's
idea of having a whole bunch of countries

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:28.160
all use the same currency. So Greece's economy
is in a disaster. A quarter of the population

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:34.860
is unemployed, and it has this very high debt
burden. Normally, if you've got really high

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.110
unemployment, what happens is that a country
makes its currency cheaper by printing extra

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:42.750
money. That makes its products cheaper on
world markets, it makes it a more attractive

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:47.719
tourist destination, and it means that foreign
investors can get great bargains. But if unemployment

00:00:47.719 --> 00:00:51.559
is really low, a country likes to have an
expensive currency. That increases people's

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:56.320
purchasing power and it keeps prices down.
And in Europe, you have a bunch of economies

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.649
that are really different. A price of Euros
that's appropriate for Greece, where they

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:04.399
have a 25% unemployment rate is way too low
for Germany, where the unemployment rate is

00:01:04.399 --> 00:01:09.930
below 5%. And Greece's problem is that it's
small, poor, and geographically isolated from

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:14.360
the rest of the Eurozone. It's like the only
vegetarian at a barbeque, except when it comes

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:18.680
to currencies, there's no side dishes. And
so there's plenty of specific decisions we

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.400
can second-guess, plenty of things Greece
did and various banks did that we can question,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.090
but fundamentally having all these countries
come to a dinner party with only one dish

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:32.710
on the menu was a mistake. The euro was a
project that Europe set about on for really

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:37.780
political reasons. It was a symbol of their
determination to have peace on the continent,

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.540
but they didn't really take the economics
of it seriously. So Greece joins the euro

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.500
in 2001 and initially, it works out great
for Greece because all of a sudden everyone

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.140
was like 'yeah, sure, let's lend them money.'
So they borrowed lots and lots and lots of

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.780
euros, except that didn't change the fact
that their economy is a lot weaker than some

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:01.729
of the other European countries. So to really
work, you would need a much, much, much closer

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:06.840
union, where you had big financial transfers
coming from the richer places to the poorer

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:12.110
places all the time. In the United States,
the poor states like Kentucky, Mississippi,

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:15.950
Alabama, they're constantly getting money
from the richer states like Massachusetts,

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:21.200
California, New York, through the welfare
system, through Social Security, through Medicare,

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.000
through Medicaid. And you know, people may
complain about this or that program, but we

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:32.410
don't dispute the idea that it's all one country
so money is going to circulate around. Europeans,

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:37.050
you know, they just don't feel that way. Germans
are willing to support poor German people,

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.900
but they don't want to support Greek people
with their tax dollars. So they're kind of

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.680
like half-way integrated in a way that doesn't
really work.

